#   [ctr_hpm_exact] mhpmcounter7 advanced 1, 0 predicted: read 00000001 -> 00000002, cycles 40..56, order 10
#   [ctr_hpm_exact] mhpmcounter8 advanced 8, 1 predicted: read 00000000 -> 00000008, cycles 49..66, order 17
#   [ctr_hpm_bound] mhpmcounter11 advanced 6, at most 1 documented (1 multiply record(s) at 1 cycles each, and 8 of the window's 13 cycles had a data access outstanding leaving 5 free): read 00000000 -> 00000006, cycles 103..116, order 39
#   [ctr_hpm_bound] mhpmcounter12 advanced 43, at most 36 documented (1 divide record(s) at 36 cycles each, and 8 of the window's 49 cycles had a data access outstanding leaving 41 free): read 00000000 -> 0000002b, cycles 162..211, order 60
# Read with the fsdb-mcp-server against dv/auto_dv/out_l65w3/build/gen_tb/vcs_simv.daidir. The B20 window was
# re-read on this build after the entry-unjudged row was added and is transition-for-transition identical to
# the read on the previous build, and the four collected messages are unchanged. Times below are the
# FSDB's own 10 ps unit and the TB clock period is 10 ns (GEN_CLK_PERIOD_NS), so one cycle is 1000.
# rvfi_ext_mhpmcounters prints index 0 first, and index 4 is mhpmcounter7 (rtl/ibex_core.sv:2108-2128).

## B20, counter 7: the fence.i is counted as a jump (b20_doc_waves)
rvfi_order                44500 = 8            49500 = 9            60500 = 10
rvfi_insn                 44500 = b07022f3     49500 = 0000100f     60500 = b0702373
#                         csrr t0, mhpmcounter7   FENCE.I              csrr t1, mhpmcounter7
perf_jump                 47500 -> 1   48500 -> 0        (one cycle, in the fence.i's own decode cycle)
rvfi_ext_mhpmcounters[4]  44500 = 00000001     49500 = 00000001     60500 = 00000002
# The window between the two reads holds exactly one instruction, the fence.i, and the counter moved from 1
# to 2 for it, with a single perf_jump pulse in its decode cycle. The documentation defines the event as
# unconditional jumps (j, jal, jr, jalr) only, which is what ctr_hpm_exact reported.
# The same window confirms the sample-point reading the read-anchored design rests on: the fence.i's pulse
# landed at the end of its own decode cycle and its OWN record still samples 1; only the next read sees 2.

## B17, counter 8: the branch counts once per cycle it waits behind the load (b17_doc_waves)
rvfi_order      53500 = 13           67500 = 14           68500 = 15
rvfi_insn       53500 = b08022f3     67500 = 0000429c     68500 = 0ec58f63
#               csrr t0, mhpmcounter8   c.lw                 BEQ
data_gnt_i      59000 -> 1   60000 -> 0        (the load is granted)
perf_branch     59500 -> 1   67500 -> 0        (EIGHT cycles high, one count per cycle)
data_rvalid_i   67000 -> 1   68000 -> 0        (the response arrives; perf_branch falls one cycle later)
# perf_branch was high for exactly the cycles the load's response was outstanding. The documentation defines
# counter 8 as one count per conditional branch, which is what ctr_hpm_exact reported.

## B17, counter 11: the multiply's wait cycles are the load's outstanding cycles (b17_doc_waves)
rvfi_insn       107500 = b0b02473    118500 = 0000469c    119500 = 02c58833    120500 = b0b024f3
#               csrr s0, mhpmcounter11  c.lw                 MUL                  csrr s1, mhpmcounter11
data_gnt_i      110000 -> 1  111000 -> 0
perf_mul_wait   111500 -> 1  118000 -> 0       (six counted cycles, ending at the response instant)
data_rvalid_i   118000 -> 1  119000 -> 0
# perf_mul_wait rose after the load was granted and fell in the SAME cycle the response arrived, so every one
# of those cycles was spent waiting for memory and none for the multiply, which under RV32MSingleCycle
# completes MD_OP_MULL in one cycle. That is the derivation the ctr_hpm_bound row rests on.
# perf_mul_wait is flat across the counter-8 window above, so the two windows are separate events.
